 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [HashNeRF](8678-hashnerf.md)
-HashNeRF is a method for representing Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) that utilizes a multiresolution hash encoding to map 3D coordinates to a higher-dimensional feature space, followed by a shallow neural network to decode density and color. It solves the problem of slow training times and high memory consumption associated with traditional NeRF models by replacing expensive positional encoding and large MLPs with a trainable, compact hash grid representation. Unlike its siblings, it achieves faster convergence and reduced parameter counts by making the encoding itself trainable alongside the shallow network, allowing it to represent 3D geometry at multiple levels of detail. See [HashNeRF](8678-hashnerf.md).
+HashNeRF accelerates NeRF training by replacing positional encoding with SMHE. It uses a shallow MLP to predict density and color from the concatenated hash features. It sets itself apart by enabling faster convergence due to the generic, trainable nature of the encoding. See [HashNeRF](8678-hashnerf.md).
 
