 ### Grid-NeRF
-Grid-NeRF uses plane grid features and a small MLP network to represent the 3D scene [S1, unofficial]. It uses a NeRF branch to guide the optimization process of the scene [S1, unofficial].
-
-### MERF Architecture
-MERF parameterizes 3D voxel grids and 2D planes using an MLP with multi-resolution hash encoding [S2]. Hash collisions are resolved with a 2-layer MLP with 64 hidden units [S2]. The output of the main MLP is an 8-dimensional vector representing density, diffuse RGB, and a 4-dimensional view-dependency feature vector [S2]. For the deferred view-dependency model, it follows SNeRG [Hedman et al. 2021] and uses a 3-layer MLP with 16 hidden units [S2]. Viewing directions are encoded with 4 frequencies [S2]. Following MipNeRF360 [Barron et al. 2022], it uses hierarchical sampling with three levels, requiring two Proposal-MLPs [S2]. The Proposal-MLPs consist of 2 layers with 64 hidden units and use a hash encoding [S2]. The hash encoding uses 20 levels, and individual hash tables have $2^{21}$ entries [S2]. The Proposal-MLPs' hash encodings use 10 levels, a maximum grid resolution of 512, and a hash table size of $2^{16}$ [S2]. To regularize the hash grids, a weight decay of 0.1 is used on its grid values [S2].
+Grid-NeRF uses plane grid features and a small MLP network to represent the 3D scene [S1, unofficial]. It utilizes a NeRF branch to guide the optimization process of the scene [S1, unofficial].
 
 ### Instant-NGP Depth Rendering
-Instant-NGP proposes a scene representation that bounds the scene into an axis-aligned bounding box using a multi-resolution hash table of trainable feature vectors, optimized via stochastic gradient descent [S3]. The features are further mapped to color and density [S3]. Since it still uses standard volume rendering, the depth in Instant-NGP can be similarly rendered as in vanilla NeRF (Eq. (2)) [S3].
+Instant-NGP bounds the scene into an axis-aligned bounding box using a multi-resolution hash table of trainable feature vectors, optimized via stochastic gradient descent [S2]. It renders depth similarly to vanilla NeRF using standard volume rendering equations [S2].
 
 ### UrbanIR Scene Representation
-UrbanIR uses two separate multi-scale hash tables for geometry and appearance to improve convergence stability and rendering quality [S4]. For a point $\mathbf{x} \in \mathbb{R}^3$, it retrieves and interpolates parameters using hash function $F(\mathbf{x}, \theta)$ [S4]. $\theta_g$ and $\theta_a$ are learnable parameters for geometry and appearance [S4]. The density field $\sigma$ is used not only for volume rendering but also for visibility estimation and normal loss calculation, requiring it to be accurate for multiple purposes [S4]. The hash encoding is implemented with tiny-cuda-nn [S4].
+UrbanIR uses two separate multi-scale hash tables for geometry and appearance to improve convergence stability and rendering quality [S3]. For a point $\mathbf{x} \in \mathbb{R}^3$, it retrieves and interpolates parameters using hash functions $F_g(\mathbf{x}, \theta_g)$ and $F_a(\mathbf{x}, \theta_a)$ [S3]. The density field $\sigma$ is used for volume rendering, visibility estimation, and normal loss calculation, requiring accuracy for multiple purposes [S3]. The implementation uses tiny-cuda-nn [S3].
 
 ## Background
-The method was introduced by Thomas Müller, Alex Evans, Christoph Schied, and Alexander Keller in "Instant Neural Graphics Primitives with a Multiresolution Hash Encoding" (SIGGRAPH 2022) [G]. It was developed to address the limitations of previous NeRF representations, specifically the high memory usage and slow training times caused by large MLPs and positional encoding [G]. The core innovation is the use of a multi-resolution hash grid to store trainable feature vectors, allowing for real-time rendering and fast training [G]. It is widely used in neural rendering applications, particularly in real-time view synthesis and large-scale scene reconstruction [S2, S3].
+The concept of multi-resolution hash encoding was introduced in the context of Instant-NGP [S2]. Instant-NGP proposed a novel scene representation that bounds an actual scene into an axis-aligned bounding box and uses a small neural network augmented by a multi-resolution hash table of trainable feature vectors whose values are optimized through stochastic gradient descent [S2]. This approach was later adopted and adapted by various other methods, including HashNeRF [S1, unofficial] and UrbanIR [S3], to accelerate training and improve scalability for large-scale scenes.
 
